    "title": "<p>Histological classification and grading of pseudomyxoma peritonei</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "J Misdraji",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<p class=\"TableTitle\"><strong>Table&nbsp;3.01</strong> Histological classification and grading of pseudomyxoma peritonei</p>\r\n<table class=\"MsoTableGridLight\" style=\"width: 703.3pt; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"0\" width=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 91.3pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"122\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Peritoneal mucinous tumour grade</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 100.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"133\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Acceptable terminology</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 88.15pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"118\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Former terminology</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.85pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"282\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Usual primary tumour<sup>a</sup></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.9pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"283\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Histological criteria</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 91.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"122\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Acellular mucin only (grade not applicable)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 100.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"133\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 88.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"118\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.85pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"282\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Grade&nbsp;1 mucinous carcinoma of diverse sites</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.9pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"283\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Acellular mucin in the peritoneal cavity without identifiable mucinous epithelial cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 91.3pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"122\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Pseudomyxoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;1</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 100.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"133\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mucinous carcinoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;1</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 88.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"118\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Disseminated peritoneal adenomucinosis</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.85pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"282\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Grade&nbsp;1 mucinous carcinoma of diverse sites</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.9pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"283\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Hypocellular mucinous deposits</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Neoplastic epithelial elements composed of strips of low-grade mucinous epithelium</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 91.3pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"122\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Pseudomyxoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;2</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 100.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"133\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mucinous carcinoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;2</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 88.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"118\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Peritoneal mucinous carcinoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.85pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"282\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">High-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Grade&nbsp;2 mucinous adenocarcinoma of diverse sites</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.9pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"283\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Hypercellular mucinous deposits as judged at 20&times;&nbsp;magnification</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">High-grade cytological features involving &gt;&nbsp;10% of the tumour</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Infiltrative-type invasion characterized by angulated glands in a desmoplastic stroma, complex glandular growth, or a pattern of numerous mucin pools containing clusters of tumour cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 91.3pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"122\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Pseudomyxoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;3</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 100.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"133\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mucinous carcinoma peritonei, grade&nbsp;3</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 88.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"118\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&nbsp;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.85pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"282\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Signet-ring cell carcinoma of diverse sites</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Appendiceal goblet cell adenocarcinoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 211.9pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"283\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mucinous tumour deposits with signet-ring cells<sup>b</sup> or sheets of tumour cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup>a</sup>Generally, the grades of the peritoneal tumour and the primary tumour that produced it are concordant. If they are not, then the grades of the primary and peritoneal tumours should be recorded individually. The grade of the peritoneal&nbsp;tumour is likely to have a greater effect on prognosis than that of the primary tumour.</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><sup>b</sup>The presence of signet-ring cells in mucin pools may be less prognostically important than signet-ring cells invading tissue. Although a lower limit for the percentage of signet-ring cells has not been set, focal signet-ring cells should be distinguished from so-called pseudo&ndash;signet-ring cells, which are degenerate single cells sloughed into mucin pools in an otherwise lower-grade tumour.</p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
